1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:01,052
Stargazing.

2
00:00:01,053 --> 00:00:11,000
Humankind's way to predict the future, to talk to the gods, and today to unlock the secrets of creation.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Our tools, magnificent machines built by pioneers pushing the limits of technology, battling personal demons and public ridicule.

4
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:27,900
Now, the great observatories from Stonehenge to Hubble

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,400
on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:45,160
The science of modern telescopes

7
00:00:45,260 --> 00:00:48,400
is more fantastic than most science fiction.

8
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,560
The sights gathered by these machines

9
00:00:50,561 --> 00:00:54,160
more spectacular than any imaginary visions.

10
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:56,900
They extend our reach to the farthest corners

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,640
of the utterly mysterious universe into which we were born.

12
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:06,500
Early man yearned for a link between the mysterious lights

13
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:10,520
in the sky and his daily life, envisioning the animals around

14
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:14,680
him as patterns in the heavens.

15
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:16,880
Thousands of years before Christ,

16
00:01:16,881 --> 00:01:18,880
peoples far removed from each other

17
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:22,120
built great stone observatories to chart the cycles

18
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:24,220
of the sun and the moon.

19
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:28,000
From Egypt to the Americas, remains of these star charts

20
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:30,900
dot the globe still.

21
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,560
So important were the structures that generations endured

22
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:36,900
backbreaking labor to complete them.

23
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:43,500
Stonehenge was the pinnacle of what that technology

24
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:45,700
was capable of producing in those times.

25
00:01:45,701 --> 00:01:49,500
in those times, was something like 20,000 man years of effort

26
00:01:49,501 --> 00:01:52,880
to move these massive blocks from North Wales all the way

27
00:01:52,881 --> 00:01:55,920
down to the south of England, a distance of several hundred

28
00:01:55,921 --> 00:01:56,720
miles.

29
00:01:56,721 --> 00:01:58,940
And this was probably without any beasts of burden

30
00:01:58,941 --> 00:01:59,940
to pull them.

31
00:01:59,941 --> 00:02:02,280
They were probably moved by hand, rolling on logs

32
00:02:02,281 --> 00:02:03,680
for that entire distance.

33
00:02:03,681 --> 00:02:05,820
So it was a huge effort and represented

34
00:02:05,821 --> 00:02:07,700
the best that that technology was

35
00:02:07,701 --> 00:02:10,020
capable of doing in terms of astronomical observatories.

36
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:14,920
For thousands of years, mankind's

37
00:02:14,921 --> 00:02:18,160
His celestial technology and his knowledge of the stars

38
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:20,460
changed little.

39
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:25,960
Then one fateful day in 1609, Dutch optician Hans Lippershey

40
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:29,160
accidentally peered through two pairs of glasses

41
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:33,300
and realized that the twin lenses magnified objects.

42
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,100
The telescope was born.

43
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:40,120
An Italian mathematics professor named Galileo Galilei

44
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:43,020
saw the instrument's military potential,

45
00:02:43,021 --> 00:02:46,520
to the Senate of Venice that the so-called spyglass

46
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:50,820
allowed incoming ships to be spotted two hours earlier

47
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:53,260
than with the naked eye.

48
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:56,960
A grateful Senate immediately doubled Galileo's salary.

49
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:05,940
In 1612, Galileo turned his spyglass skyward

50
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:11,880
and instantly saw 10 times further than any previous human.

51
00:03:11,881 --> 00:03:14,280
In time, his pioneering observations

52
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:17,820
would reveal that the Earth rotates around the sun,

53
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:21,320
directly contradicting age-old beliefs that the Earth

54
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:23,920
is the center of the universe.

55
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:26,600
The Catholic Church was not amused.

56
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:29,660
Galileo was brought before the Holy Roman Inquisition

57
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:33,000
to stand trial for heresy.

58
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:36,280
Under threat of torture, he publicly reaffirmed the Church's

59
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:39,380
belief that the Earth is immovable.

60
00:03:39,381 --> 00:03:42,020
But as he was led away to what would be house arrest

61
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:45,080
for the rest of his life and eventual blindness

62
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:48,860
caused by staring at the sun, he whispered,

63
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:52,360
,, but it does move.

64
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:05,380
The year 1868 saw the birth of a man like Galileo

65
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:07,140
who would devote his heart and soul

66
00:04:07,141 --> 00:04:11,240
to the advancement of astronomy, George Ellery Hale.

67
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:15,080
As a boy, Hale read Jules Verne's From the Earth to the Moon

68
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:16,780
and was hooked on astronomy.

69
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:25,300
By age 13, he was working with professional astronomers.

70
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:27,800
Hale's self-appointed mission was to build

71
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:32,840
ever more powerful telescopes, machines of staggering size,

72
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:35,640
and exquisite precision that would unveil

73
00:04:35,641 --> 00:04:38,380
the secrets of the universe.

74
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:41,540
To accomplish this goal, he would brave a never-ending

75
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:45,420
series of obstacles, including a lifelong struggle

76
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:48,020
with mental illness.

77
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:50,580
At times, the demons were literally crippling.

78
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:53,320
He would wake up in the night and have a sense

79
00:04:53,420 --> 00:04:55,660
of little demons coming and talking to him

80
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:57,700
and telling him his life was worthless and everything he had

81
00:04:57,800 --> 00:04:59,200
done was worthless.

82
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:02,760
And much of his life was a battle between these periods

83
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:04,800
of extraordinary clarity.

84
00:05:04,801 --> 00:05:07,400
when he was able to do what perhaps no other scientist

85
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:09,540
or organizer of science could do,

86
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:12,740
and periods when he was almost incapacitated by these demons,

87
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:14,440
when he would have to retire to a dark room.

88
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:21,620
The dawn of the 20th century was an era of competition

89
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:25,580
to build bigger and better machines, ships, buildings,

90
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:26,760
trains.

91
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:32,820
Engineers raced to create more spectacular versions of each.

92
00:05:32,821 --> 00:05:35,320
Hale took advantage of this expansive spirit

93
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:38,560
to raise money for his epic projects.

94
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:41,160
In 1907, he began a decade-long effort

95
00:05:41,260 --> 00:05:45,340
to create a telescope with a 100-inch diameter mirror,

96
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:49,200
by far the most ambitious ever attempted.

97
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:52,300
This instrument, if only he could find a way to build it,

98
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:56,080
could hold the answers to ancient questions about the size

99
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:58,120
and age of the universe.

100
00:06:02,820 --> 00:06:05,920
Hale took industrialist Andrew Carnegie by the hand,

101
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:08,320
whining and dining him, demonstrating how

102
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:12,160
telescopes work, awing him with the wondrous discoveries

103
00:06:12,260 --> 00:06:12,960
to come.

104
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:18,900
The Carnegie Foundation did provide the needed funds,

105
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,880
and over the next years, a dome arose atop remote Mount Wilson

106
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:26,880
near Pasadena, California.

107
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:30,920
Chosen for its clear air and distance from manmade lights,

108
00:06:30,921 --> 00:06:34,220
Mount Wilson would see workmen converge by the hundreds

109
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:38,960
to transform Hale's dream into a reality of glass and steel.

110
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:46,940
The Great Observatory was finally finished in 1917,

111
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,340
but only the first sighting through the telescope

112
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:53,580
known as First Light would reveal whether it worked.

113
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:56,740
On a cold November night, Hale led 20 colleagues

114
00:06:56,840 --> 00:06:59,920
across a narrow wooden bridge to the dome.

115
00:06:59,921 --> 00:07:03,020
With them was poet Alfred Noyes, on hand

116
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:06,560
to record the historic occasion of first light

117
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:08,960
at the world's mightiest telescope.

118
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:15,160
Where was the gambler that would stake so much time,

119
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:18,880
patience, treasure on a single throw?

120
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:23,080
All their youth was fuel to the flame of this one work.

121
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:25,280
One in a lifetime to the man of science,

122
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:27,580
there comes this drama.

123
00:07:27,581 --> 00:07:31,580
If he fails, he fails utterly.

124
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:36,120
Alfred Noyes, Watchers of the Night.

125
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:39,600
Hale, who had aged from 39 to 49 over the course

126
00:07:39,700 --> 00:07:43,760
of the project, was given the honor of first view

127
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:46,640
and turned the telescope on Jupiter.

128
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:50,500
To his horror, not one but several overlapping images

129
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:52,440
filled the eyepiece.

130
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:56,140
The 100-inch mirror appeared fatally flawed.

131
00:07:56,141 --> 00:07:59,440
Hale's 10-year obsession an utter failure.

132
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:02,740
But then a colleague recalled that workmen had left the dome

133
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:03,840
open during the day.

134
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:07,380
Perhaps residual heat was distorting the mirror.

135
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:11,880
Hale must wait hours longer for the mirror to cool.

136
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:14,200
And for those few hours, no one is quite sure

137
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:16,700
whether they have a total dud on their hands

138
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:18,900
or a magnificent new telescope.

139
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,140
Hale and Walter Adams don't even sleep

140
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:23,940
until they come back two and a half hours later,

141
00:08:23,941 --> 00:08:26,880
come back in, spin the telescope around to a new target,

142
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:29,720
look in, and sure enough, see perhaps the clearest and most

143
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:31,920
brilliant image anyone had ever seen through a telescope.

144
00:08:45,300 --> 00:08:48,200
Hale had built the 100-inch telescope,

145
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:51,940
but it was the observations of astronomer Edwin Hubble

146
00:08:51,941 --> 00:08:54,080
that would make it famous.

147
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:57,340
High school basketball star, Rhodes Scholar, and World War

148
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:01,420
I infantry veteran, Edwin Hubble gave up the study of law

149
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:05,360
for astronomy because, as he said, astronomy matters.

150
00:09:08,860 --> 00:09:11,720
Hubble affected in almost every circumstance

151
00:09:11,820 --> 00:09:12,720
a British accent.

152
00:09:12,820 --> 00:09:16,600
He wasn't at all British, a pipe, a tweed jacket,

153
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:19,260
and a set of mannerisms that were really, if you want,

154
00:09:19,261 --> 00:09:21,960
English common room and sherry sipping.

155
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:23,500
It didn't win him a lot of friends

156
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:26,360
among fellow astronomers.

157
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:29,000
But no one questioned Hubble's intellect

158
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:31,100
or his determination.

159
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:34,900
Night after grueling night, he guided the 100-inch telescope,

160
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,620
exposing single photographs for hours,

161
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:41,080
testing his commitment, his weary eyes,

162
00:09:41,180 --> 00:09:43,620
and his bladder control.

163
00:09:43,720 --> 00:09:45,820
And guiding means you have to sit with your eye

164
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:48,260
at an eyepiece, watching a guide star,

165
00:09:48,261 --> 00:09:52,260
keep it on a crosshair constantly throughout the entire exposure.

166
00:09:52,261 --> 00:09:53,260
And you're stuck there.

167
00:09:53,261 --> 00:09:55,260
And in the winter, you are really stuck

168
00:09:55,261 --> 00:09:57,260
because it's very cold at most observatories.

169
00:10:00,260 --> 00:10:03,260
Within a few years, Hubble's photographs answered

170
00:10:03,261 --> 00:10:06,260
the great astronomical question of the age,

171
00:10:06,261 --> 00:10:11,260
proving that the Milky Way is just one of innumerable galaxies.

172
00:10:11,261 --> 00:10:15,260
Our local cluster of stars long thought to contain the entire universe

173
00:10:15,261 --> 00:10:19,740
was but a small outpost, a drop in the cosmic ocean.

174
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:22,860
And not only was the universe far larger than previously

175
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:27,640
imagined, it was expanding at fantastic speeds.

176
00:10:27,740 --> 00:10:29,980
Edwin Hubble had discovered the first clues

177
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:34,340
to solving the ultimate mystery, evidence that all we know

178
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:37,720
or ever will know began in a big bang.

179
00:10:37,721 --> 00:10:38,620
Bang.

180
00:10:44,620 --> 00:10:47,000
Hubble's work on the 100-inch telescope

181
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:49,600
had revealed new depths to the universe.

182
00:10:49,700 --> 00:10:52,060
But it would take an even mightier observatory

183
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:54,100
to explore those depths.

184
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:57,740
Master builder George Hale stood ready to meet the challenge.

185
00:11:00,480 --> 00:11:03,580
In 200 BC, Eratosthenes estimated

186
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:07,720
the circumference of the Earth with over 99% accuracy.

187
00:11:07,721 --> 00:11:11,160
by measuring the moving shadow cask by a stick in the ground.

188
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:18,360
In the 1920s, George Hale set his sights on creating a machine

189
00:11:18,361 --> 00:11:23,500
capable of seeing a candle at 10,000 miles, 10 million times

190
00:11:23,501 --> 00:11:26,500
more sensitive than the human eye.

191
00:11:26,501 --> 00:11:29,000
Only with a massive 200-inch telescope

192
00:11:29,001 --> 00:11:31,980
could scientists explore the vast universe

193
00:11:31,981 --> 00:11:35,220
that Hubble had glimpsed.

194
00:11:35,221 --> 00:11:38,960
The largest scientific instrument ever built, the 200 inch,

195
00:11:39,060 --> 00:11:41,920
would weigh over 500 tons.

196
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:45,500
Not simply a bigger version of earlier observatories,

197
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:48,340
it was instead an entirely new beast,

198
00:11:48,440 --> 00:11:54,600
requiring revolutionary advances in a host of technologies.

199
00:11:54,700 --> 00:11:57,880
But Hale was 60 years old and in perilous health,

200
00:11:57,980 --> 00:12:01,380
increasingly incapacitated by mental breakdowns

201
00:12:01,480 --> 00:12:03,620
he had no time to lose.

202
00:12:03,621 --> 00:12:07,100
He zeroed in on his old friend Wycliffe Rose, a man

203
00:12:07,200 --> 00:12:11,120
fond of saying, make the peaks higher.

204
00:12:11,220 --> 00:12:13,600
Together, they persuaded the Rockefeller Foundation

205
00:12:13,700 --> 00:12:17,700
to provide $6 million, the biggest grant ever

206
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:20,380
for a scientific project.

207
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:24,300
This was the birth of big science in America,

208
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:27,140
the beginning of a huge nationwide effort to study

209
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:28,440
nature.

210
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:31,920
When Hale announced the project in October 1928,

211
00:12:31,921 --> 00:12:35,420
newspapers around the globe went wild with excitement.

212
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:39,260
They immediately dubbed the telescope the Giant Eye

213
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:43,460
and proclaimed it a landmark event in human history.

214
00:12:43,560 --> 00:12:46,660
Standing on the threshold of a vast, uncharted space

215
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:49,680
to be penetrated by the 200-inch telescope,

216
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:53,280
the scientific world is frankly a tiptoe with excitement.

217
00:12:53,380 --> 00:12:57,580
What, men are asking themselves, will the gigantic new telescope

218
00:12:57,680 --> 00:12:58,580
reveal?

219
00:12:58,581 --> 00:13:03,820
Los Angeles Times, October 29, 1928.

220
00:13:03,821 --> 00:13:06,920
And the public enthusiasm that followed was just amazing.

221
00:13:06,921 --> 00:13:09,220
It went from a little girl sending a dollar

222
00:13:09,221 --> 00:13:11,620
that she had saved up from her allowance

223
00:13:11,621 --> 00:13:15,740
to people congratulating Hale on, in a sense, reinventing

224
00:13:15,741 --> 00:13:19,140
the world, beginning our great age of science and achievement

225
00:13:19,141 --> 00:13:20,740
all over again.

226
00:13:20,741 --> 00:13:24,040
I think there's probably never been a response quite as

227
00:13:24,041 --> 00:13:28,480
enthusiastic and as overwhelming toward an instrument

228
00:13:28,481 --> 00:13:29,780
with such a benign purpose.

229
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:43,360
When Hale predicted that the new telescope

230
00:13:43,460 --> 00:13:45,560
could take seven years to complete,

231
00:13:45,660 --> 00:13:47,400
he was called a pessimist.

232
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:50,500
But he knew better than anyone that every stage of the project

233
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:53,240
called for research and engineering never before

234
00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:54,600
attempted.

235
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:56,880
Many doubted whether it was even possible

236
00:13:56,881 --> 00:13:58,620
to make such an enormous mirror.

237
00:14:01,520 --> 00:14:04,580
Nobody knew you could cast a piece of glass 200 inches

238
00:14:04,680 --> 00:14:06,520
in diameter successfully.

239
00:14:06,620 --> 00:14:08,820
And of course, without that, there is no telescope.

240
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:12,660
The glass, the 200-inch mirror, is the light collecting part

241
00:14:12,760 --> 00:14:15,560
of the telescope and the focusing element of the telescope.

242
00:14:15,660 --> 00:14:19,100
And if that doesn't work, nothing works.

243
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:22,400
Creating a large mirror involves pouring hundreds

244
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:25,600
of buckets of molten glass into a mold.

245
00:14:25,601 --> 00:14:28,400
In this case, a 200-inch mold.

246
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:30,800
The resulting disk must be free of bubbles

247
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:32,800
or other imperfections.

248
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:35,040
Corning Glass Works in Corning, New York,

249
00:14:35,140 --> 00:14:38,080
experimented with a new substance for this mirror,

250
00:14:38,180 --> 00:14:39,840
Pyrex glass.

251
00:14:39,940 --> 00:14:42,820
In March 1934, journalist Lowell Thomas

252
00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:46,920
used his national radio program to inform America

253
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:51,800
that Corning was ready to create the 200-inch mirror.

254
00:14:51,900 --> 00:14:53,700
He heard about this mirror pouring,

255
00:14:53,701 --> 00:14:55,700
and in one of his nightly news broadcasts,

256
00:14:55,701 --> 00:14:59,500
announced in the typical Lowell Thomas hyperbole

257
00:14:59,501 --> 00:15:03,500
that this was the single greatest event of the 20th century,

258
00:15:03,501 --> 00:15:07,500
and by which he did not mean even to exclude the First World War.

259
00:15:07,501 --> 00:15:10,500
And from that moment, the world just turned toward Corning

260
00:15:10,501 --> 00:15:13,000
and poured into Corning to see this event,

261
00:15:13,001 --> 00:15:16,000
and it became an incredible publicity show.

262
00:15:16,001 --> 00:15:19,000
People in the galleries would, during the pouring, be singing,

263
00:15:19,001 --> 00:15:22,000
I'm seeing the world through rose-colored glasses.

264
00:15:22,001 --> 00:15:24,540
They had everything but an orchestra playing

265
00:15:24,640 --> 00:15:26,400
during this very delicate operation

266
00:15:26,500 --> 00:15:28,900
of trying to pour the largest chunk of glass ever poured.

267
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:33,440
Only days later would the audience

268
00:15:33,540 --> 00:15:36,240
learn that the pour was a failure.

269
00:15:36,340 --> 00:15:39,680
Dozens of cords anchoring the disc in a honeycomb pattern

270
00:15:39,780 --> 00:15:41,220
had broken loose.

271
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:43,380
Reattachment was impossible.

272
00:15:43,480 --> 00:15:46,600
Nine months later, Corning poured another disc,

273
00:15:46,700 --> 00:15:50,660
this time without spectators.

274
00:15:50,661 --> 00:15:53,600
But casting the mirror was just the first step.

275
00:15:53,700 --> 00:15:56,860
To prevent scarring, the 1,500 degree molten mass

276
00:15:56,960 --> 00:16:00,300
was cooled by less than 1 degree a day,

277
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:03,040
a process that would take 10 months.

278
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:05,580
After six months of controlled cooling,

279
00:16:05,680 --> 00:16:08,980
the worst flooding in decades struck Corning.

280
00:16:09,080 --> 00:16:11,440
When the Chemung River overflowed its banks,

281
00:16:11,540 --> 00:16:14,520
rainwater surged towards the Corning factories.

282
00:16:14,620 --> 00:16:16,960
If power was lost, so was the mirror.

283
00:16:20,660 --> 00:16:24,260
The water level slowly rose, and despite all the efforts

284
00:16:24,360 --> 00:16:27,060
and all the pumping trucks and all the men of Corning

285
00:16:27,160 --> 00:16:30,300
with jackhammers and sandbags, the water literally

286
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:32,400
lapped to the top of the transformer.

287
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:34,100
They got the one transformer they

288
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:36,700
needed to keep the project alive out, really

289
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:38,720
with about a minute to spare.

290
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:41,920
And had that not happened, the mirror would have been lost.

291
00:16:42,020 --> 00:16:46,320
In 1936, the 40,000 pound chunk of glass

292
00:16:46,420 --> 00:16:50,660
was shipped west to the Caltech optical shop in Pasadena.

293
00:16:50,661 --> 00:16:54,900
Everywhere it went, the train was literally mobbed by people.

294
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:56,700
There would be towns where the temperature

295
00:16:56,800 --> 00:16:59,640
was 20 degrees below zero, and all the schools were let out

296
00:16:59,740 --> 00:17:01,900
so the children could line the tracks

297
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,980
and see this achievement of American technology go by.

298
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:07,420
This was what we could do.

299
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:08,740
This was achievement.

300
00:17:08,840 --> 00:17:11,820
This was the best of America.

301
00:17:11,920 --> 00:17:14,320
Even the Lindbergh baby kidnapping trial

302
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:16,460
was knocked off the nation's front pages

303
00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:19,420
by news of the Mirror's journey west.

304
00:17:19,421 --> 00:17:23,420
George Hale's dream had become a part of American folklore.

305
00:17:28,420 --> 00:17:33,100
In April 1936, the Great Disk arrived in California.

306
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:36,440
As locals rode shotgun down the streets of Pasadena,

307
00:17:36,540 --> 00:17:40,180
the mirror was escorted to the Caltech Optical Lab.

308
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:43,520
In the excitement of the day, no one could have imagined

309
00:17:43,620 --> 00:17:46,080
that completion of the telescope still

310
00:17:46,180 --> 00:17:49,420
lay more than a decade away.

311
00:17:49,421 --> 00:17:55,420
While the public focused on the giant mirror,

312
00:17:55,520 --> 00:17:58,420
Hale and his colleagues turned to the many other challenges

313
00:17:58,520 --> 00:18:00,460
of creating an observatory.

314
00:18:00,560 --> 00:18:04,760
The dome, the telescope tube, the rotating device,

315
00:18:04,860 --> 00:18:07,480
all presented major obstacles.

316
00:18:07,580 --> 00:18:10,140
By the mid-1930s, Hale's brainchild

317
00:18:10,240 --> 00:18:13,820
occupied thousands of engineers, mechanics, and workmen

318
00:18:13,920 --> 00:18:16,480
from coast to coast.

319
00:18:16,481 --> 00:18:20,380
Mount Palomar outside San Diego was chosen as the site

320
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:22,080
for the observatory.

321
00:18:22,180 --> 00:18:25,120
At 5,000 feet above sea level, Palomar

322
00:18:25,220 --> 00:18:27,520
is blessed with some of the clearest skies

323
00:18:27,620 --> 00:18:29,520
in the northern hemisphere.

324
00:18:29,620 --> 00:18:32,520
And appropriate enough, in Indian legend,

325
00:18:32,620 --> 00:18:35,960
the mountain served as a departure point for souls

326
00:18:36,060 --> 00:18:37,400
journeying to heaven.

327
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:43,240
In Philadelphia, Westinghouse proudly

328
00:18:43,241 --> 00:18:48,240
unveiled the 50-foot-long, 150,000-pound tube designed

329
00:18:48,340 --> 00:18:50,680
to hold the mirror.

330
00:18:50,780 --> 00:18:53,780
This tube would eventually be mounted on a horseshoe bearing

331
00:18:53,880 --> 00:18:58,120
weighing 400,000 pounds, the heaviest single unit ever

332
00:18:58,220 --> 00:18:59,560
machined.

333
00:18:59,660 --> 00:19:02,520
Even when broken down into component pieces,

334
00:19:02,620 --> 00:19:05,260
the hardware was too big to ship by land.

335
00:19:05,261 --> 00:19:09,260
The giant steel blocks were loaded onto cargo vessels

336
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:13,740
and taken by sea through the Panama Canal and up to California.

337
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:16,740
These and 1,000 other difficulties

338
00:19:16,840 --> 00:19:20,640
were conquered by Hale's leadership.

339
00:19:20,740 --> 00:19:24,740
He is, in effect, a one-person network,

340
00:19:24,840 --> 00:19:28,820
bringing together industry, academia, government,

341
00:19:28,920 --> 00:19:31,760
scientists, and forcing them to work together

342
00:19:31,860 --> 00:19:34,420
in a way to help them to help them

343
00:19:34,421 --> 00:19:36,380
in a way that has never occurred beforehand.

344
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:40,360
Today, we have an agency like NASA or the National Science

345
00:19:40,460 --> 00:19:43,160
Foundation that spends all of its time doing exactly that.

346
00:19:43,260 --> 00:19:44,860
But George Hale is accomplishing that when there

347
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:46,560
are no support structures.

348
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:49,260
There are no institutions to do it.

349
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:52,480
The Palomar Dome was the largest welded structure

350
00:19:52,580 --> 00:19:57,280
ever built, and every part of it demanded exact precision.

351
00:19:57,380 --> 00:19:59,180
The rails upon which it would rotate

352
00:19:59,280 --> 00:20:02,880
had to be smooth within 1,000th of an inch.

353
00:20:02,881 --> 00:20:07,020
Anything less would introduce a jitter into the photographed

354
00:20:07,120 --> 00:20:08,120
images.

355
00:20:08,220 --> 00:20:11,660
Only after six months and thousands of man hours of

356
00:20:11,760 --> 00:20:16,500
smoothing were the rails pronounced ready.

357
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:18,720
The moving parts of the 200-inch telescope

358
00:20:18,820 --> 00:20:20,760
go 535 tons.

359
00:20:20,860 --> 00:20:24,500
And we talk about tolerances of 1,000ths of an inch.

360
00:20:24,600 --> 00:20:28,000
To see that kind of precision in that size machine

361
00:20:28,100 --> 00:20:29,500
is a remarkable combination.

362
00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:35,880
The biggest enterprise in scientific history

363
00:20:35,980 --> 00:20:39,480
had grown beyond the longevity of its creators.

364
00:20:39,580 --> 00:20:42,920
In 1938, Francis Pease, the original designer

365
00:20:43,020 --> 00:20:46,820
of both the 100-inch and the 200-inch telescopes,

366
00:20:46,920 --> 00:20:50,300
died from complications of cancer surgery.

367
00:20:50,400 --> 00:20:53,140
And George Hale was failing fast.

368
00:20:53,240 --> 00:20:56,400
Now confined to a wheelchair, he approached the end

369
00:20:56,500 --> 00:20:58,300
with serenity.

370
00:20:58,400 --> 00:21:02,200
On February 18, 1938, the world was

371
00:21:02,201 --> 00:21:06,440
1938, he was wheeled outside, where he looked up at the sky

372
00:21:06,540 --> 00:21:09,340
and said, it is a beautiful day.

373
00:21:09,440 --> 00:21:13,380
The sun is shining, and they are working on Palomar.

374
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:16,280
Just days later, he died at age 69.

375
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:26,360
Hale's colleagues continued to drive the project forward.

376
00:21:26,460 --> 00:21:30,620
As ever, the toughest challenge was perfecting the giant mirror.

377
00:21:30,621 --> 00:21:33,920
In the Kel-Tec Optical Shop, 20 technicians

378
00:21:34,020 --> 00:21:37,500
operated in the ultimate clean room.

379
00:21:37,600 --> 00:21:40,900
If the slightest microscopic speck fell under a polishing

380
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,600
brush, the mirror would be scratched,

381
00:21:43,700 --> 00:21:47,800
setting the process back months or even years.

382
00:21:47,900 --> 00:21:50,940
Marcus Brown, the single man connected with the telescope

383
00:21:51,040 --> 00:21:54,280
from origin to completion, protected his mirror

384
00:21:54,380 --> 00:22:00,620
with the zeal of a Secret Service agent guarding the president.

385
00:22:00,621 --> 00:22:03,500
Some people thought that Marcus Brown was almost a fanatic

386
00:22:03,501 --> 00:22:04,700
in terms of his disk.

387
00:22:04,900 --> 00:22:05,880
He would order a cleaning.

388
00:22:06,160 --> 00:22:07,300
As soon as they finished the cleaning,

389
00:22:07,380 --> 00:22:09,440
he would order them to clean the entire laboratory again.

390
00:22:10,060 --> 00:22:11,520
But he had become possessive.

391
00:22:11,600 --> 00:22:14,380
He was a man with a calling, and this was his masterpiece.

392
00:22:15,340 --> 00:22:18,160
And he ruled his optic shop as a dictator.

393
00:22:19,740 --> 00:22:22,880
Month after month, year after year,

394
00:22:22,881 --> 00:22:27,020
the 200-inch disk turned in a never-ending circle,

395
00:22:27,420 --> 00:22:29,660
polished with ever finer tools.

396
00:22:29,661 --> 00:22:33,900
Over five tons of glass were removed at the optical shop.

397
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:38,600
As the 1930s came to a close, the mirror approached its goal,

398
00:22:38,700 --> 00:22:44,300
two millionth of an inch from optical perfection.

399
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:48,320
If you want to understand how smoothly polished the glass is,

400
00:22:48,420 --> 00:22:52,020
try to imagine scaling that 15-foot mirror up to the size

401
00:22:52,120 --> 00:22:53,420
of the United States.

402
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:56,920
If you do that, the highest mountains that the glass would

403
00:22:57,020 --> 00:22:59,420
have are something on the order of six inches or six.

404
00:22:59,421 --> 00:23:01,820
It's an extremely well-polished piece of glass.

405
00:23:06,820 --> 00:23:10,160
In 1940, with the mirror on the verge of completion,

406
00:23:10,260 --> 00:23:14,060
the entire project fell victim to the demands of war.

407
00:23:14,160 --> 00:23:17,580
Workers and facilities were turned over to the war effort.

408
00:23:17,680 --> 00:23:21,180
The precious mirror crated up and put into storage.

409
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:25,020
The noble search for answers to the riddles of the universe

410
00:23:25,021 --> 00:23:29,500
would have to pause before the barbarity of mankind.

411
00:23:45,740 --> 00:23:48,280
After the war, the mirror was brought out of storage

412
00:23:48,380 --> 00:23:51,820
and the task of polishing its surface resumed.

413
00:23:51,821 --> 00:23:57,660
Finally, in late 1947, after 11 years and 180,000 man hours

414
00:23:57,760 --> 00:24:02,560
of grinding and polishing, the job was done.

415
00:24:02,660 --> 00:24:06,360
The 200-inch mirror was ready for its final journey

416
00:24:06,460 --> 00:24:08,940
to the top of Mount Palomar.

417
00:24:09,040 --> 00:24:12,380
Bellier Truck Company, who guaranteed to tackle anything

418
00:24:12,480 --> 00:24:15,480
from a building to a whale, was entrusted

419
00:24:15,580 --> 00:24:17,480
with the priceless cargo.

420
00:24:17,580 --> 00:24:20,980
A caravan of trucks escorted by 10 highway patrolmen

421
00:24:20,981 --> 00:24:25,980
crawled forward at a maximum 10 miles an hour.

422
00:24:25,981 --> 00:24:28,480
In the early morning hours, this cargo goes by.

423
00:24:28,481 --> 00:24:30,480
It's not been announced anywhere.

424
00:24:30,481 --> 00:24:32,980
And farmers first wake up, and other people wake up

425
00:24:32,981 --> 00:24:36,480
and see something go by, and it's not clear what it is even.

426
00:24:36,481 --> 00:24:38,480
And then each village would go through,

427
00:24:38,481 --> 00:24:41,480
each juncture on the street, someone would say,

428
00:24:41,481 --> 00:24:42,980
it's the Giant Eye.

429
00:24:42,981 --> 00:24:44,480
There would be a sort of hush,

430
00:24:44,481 --> 00:24:46,480
because people would have a sense

431
00:24:46,481 --> 00:24:50,480
that they would never again see anything quite like this.

432
00:24:50,481 --> 00:24:57,480
The 200-inch telescope had survived a depression, a world

433
00:24:57,580 --> 00:25:00,460
war, and the death of its creators.

434
00:25:00,560 --> 00:25:03,220
Two full decades, a human generation

435
00:25:03,320 --> 00:25:08,340
had passed between financing and completion, a long time

436
00:25:08,440 --> 00:25:11,040
in human terms, but insignificant

437
00:25:11,140 --> 00:25:13,440
on an astronomical timescale.

438
00:25:15,500 --> 00:25:20,480
On June 3, 1948, with George Hale's widow in Italy,

439
00:25:20,481 --> 00:25:23,220
the telescope was officially dedicated.

440
00:25:26,320 --> 00:25:29,020
As this great instrument probes the secrets

441
00:25:29,120 --> 00:25:32,420
of the universe, it is fitting that it should stand also

442
00:25:32,520 --> 00:25:36,660
in memory of the great scientist who contributed so brilliantly

443
00:25:36,760 --> 00:25:41,240
to the science of astronomy and who served so ably his community

444
00:25:41,340 --> 00:25:42,700
and his nation.

445
00:25:42,800 --> 00:25:46,300
Dr. Lee DeBridge, dedicating the Hale Telescope.

446
00:25:50,480 --> 00:25:55,480
For 30 years, the Hale telescope

447
00:25:55,580 --> 00:26:00,460
would reign supreme as the finest observatory on Earth.

448
00:26:00,560 --> 00:26:04,860
Much of the history of 20th century cosmology and astronomy

449
00:26:04,960 --> 00:26:07,240
was written at Palomar.

450
00:26:07,340 --> 00:26:10,200
On this mountaintop, man for the first time

451
00:26:10,300 --> 00:26:13,340
saw the life and deaths of stars.

452
00:26:13,440 --> 00:26:17,500
It was here that the universe was revealed as twice larger

453
00:26:17,501 --> 00:26:20,140
larger than previously imagined.

454
00:26:20,240 --> 00:26:22,900
And even today, the telescope contributes daily

455
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:26,240
to its continuing legacy.

456
00:26:26,340 --> 00:26:28,540
The 200-inch telescope and all optical telescopes

457
00:26:28,640 --> 00:26:32,940
in the world are always kept at the most critical edge

458
00:26:33,040 --> 00:26:35,060
of research capabilities.

459
00:26:35,160 --> 00:26:38,860
That was built into them by the people who designed it.

460
00:26:38,960 --> 00:26:42,600
It always amazes me how well-built the 200-inch telescope

461
00:26:42,700 --> 00:26:43,500
is.

462
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:46,200
It was built to last.

463
00:26:46,201 --> 00:26:49,200
The magnificent new machine came too late

464
00:26:49,300 --> 00:26:54,340
for the great astronomer Edwin Hubble, who died in 1953.

465
00:26:54,440 --> 00:26:59,040
But Hubble's name would endure, inspiring the next great leap

466
00:26:59,140 --> 00:27:03,760
in man's quest to know the heavens.

467
00:27:03,860 --> 00:27:06,160
Using the Palomar Telescope, astronomers

468
00:27:06,260 --> 00:27:12,200
have discovered quasars up to 12 billion light years from Earth.

469
00:27:12,300 --> 00:27:14,860
From our home on Earth, we look out

470
00:27:14,861 --> 00:27:19,960
Look out into the distance and strive to imagine the sort of world into which we were born.

471
00:27:20,960 --> 00:27:22,820
The search will continue.

472
00:27:23,680 --> 00:27:25,820
The urge is older than history.

473
00:27:26,380 --> 00:27:29,360
It is not satisfied and it will not be suppressed.

474
00:27:30,180 --> 00:27:31,020
Edwin Hubble.

475
00:27:32,240 --> 00:27:37,460
By the 1970s, astronomy was ready for its next leap in technology.

476
00:27:38,260 --> 00:27:41,680
The main limitation on all telescopes, even the hail,

477
00:27:41,681 --> 00:27:45,320
was distortion introduced by the Earth's atmosphere.

478
00:27:45,420 --> 00:27:49,720
The very twinkling of the stars is caused by this unsteady air.

479
00:27:49,820 --> 00:27:52,820
The solution, place an orbiting observatory

480
00:27:52,920 --> 00:27:54,920
high above the atmosphere.

481
00:27:55,020 --> 00:27:57,920
With a clear view of the stars, a space telescope

482
00:27:58,020 --> 00:28:00,240
would act as the eyes of the Earth.

483
00:28:00,340 --> 00:28:03,440
It could see to the very edge of the universe

484
00:28:03,540 --> 00:28:05,540
and the dawn of creation.

485
00:28:05,541 --> 00:28:10,780
What was soon named the Hubble Space Telescope

486
00:28:10,880 --> 00:28:13,920
grew into the most complex scientific instrument

487
00:28:14,020 --> 00:28:15,180
ever built.

488
00:28:15,280 --> 00:28:17,480
More than just a telescope, Hubble

489
00:28:17,580 --> 00:28:20,380
is actually a full-scale observatory,

490
00:28:20,480 --> 00:28:24,260
complete with cameras, computers, and a guidance system.

491
00:28:24,360 --> 00:28:28,060
Throughout the 1980s, over 10,000 people around the globe

492
00:28:28,160 --> 00:28:29,960
worked to create it.

493
00:28:30,060 --> 00:28:33,900
The European Space Agency built two giant solar arrays

494
00:28:33,901 --> 00:28:39,300
to power the craft, each panel containing 48,800 postage

495
00:28:39,400 --> 00:28:42,540
sized solar cells.

496
00:28:42,640 --> 00:28:44,980
Computers in Connecticut and California

497
00:28:45,080 --> 00:28:49,580
monitored polishing of the 94 inch primary mirror.

498
00:28:49,680 --> 00:28:52,580
Hubble's operations ground system, its brain,

499
00:28:52,680 --> 00:28:56,620
consumed 12,000 man years of computer programming.

500
00:28:56,621 --> 00:29:01,060
programming.

501
00:29:01,160 --> 00:29:05,200
Although a hefty 13 tons, Hubble is actually

502
00:29:05,300 --> 00:29:07,460
the most fragile of machines.

503
00:29:07,560 --> 00:29:11,560
On April 24, 1990, the space shuttle Discovery,

504
00:29:11,660 --> 00:29:16,140
with Hubble aboard, counted down to liftoff.

505
00:29:16,240 --> 00:29:19,140
Around the world, astronomers crossed their fingers.

506
00:29:22,040 --> 00:29:24,840
What you do is you build your telescope, the best way you know

507
00:29:24,841 --> 00:29:28,840
and then you put it on top of a controlled explosion

508
00:29:28,841 --> 00:29:31,840
and hope that it'll make it to orbit all right.

509
00:29:31,841 --> 00:29:34,840
So for me, the biggest risk, and it was a nail-biting time,

510
00:29:34,841 --> 00:29:38,840
during the launch was that something drastic would go wrong.

511
00:29:38,841 --> 00:29:43,840
3, 2, 1, and liftoff of the space shuttle Discovery

512
00:29:43,841 --> 00:29:49,840
with the Hubble Space Telescope, our window on the universe.

513
00:29:49,841 --> 00:29:51,840
When Hubble launched in April of 1990,

514
00:29:51,841 --> 00:29:54,180
It was a dream come true because many of us

515
00:29:54,280 --> 00:29:57,180
have worked on this program for decades, some even longer

516
00:29:57,280 --> 00:29:58,680
than 920 years.

517
00:29:58,780 --> 00:30:02,480
And just seeing the shuttle going up there was just incredible

518
00:30:02,580 --> 00:30:03,280
high.

519
00:30:03,380 --> 00:30:05,480
But then the problem started.

520
00:30:05,580 --> 00:30:08,220
NASA's plan called for the shuttle's long arm

521
00:30:08,320 --> 00:30:10,600
to lift Hubble into space.

522
00:30:10,700 --> 00:30:12,800
And everyone knew that this deployment

523
00:30:12,900 --> 00:30:14,800
was a period of maximum risk.

524
00:30:17,100 --> 00:30:19,400
Once in high orbit, the Discovery crew

525
00:30:19,401 --> 00:30:23,240
set about carefully freeing the $2 billion machine

526
00:30:23,340 --> 00:30:24,780
and its cargo bay.

527
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:29,040
Discovery, you're go for umbilical disconnect.

528
00:30:29,140 --> 00:30:32,440
The slightest mistake could imperil the telescope.

529
00:30:32,540 --> 00:30:34,440
As if that were not pressure enough,

530
00:30:34,540 --> 00:30:38,980
the astronauts were forced to operate under a tight deadline.

531
00:30:39,080 --> 00:30:42,120
Unless the solar arrays were up and running in time,

532
00:30:42,220 --> 00:30:45,320
Hubble would be no more.

533
00:30:45,420 --> 00:30:47,320
We had to get those out and powered up

534
00:30:47,321 --> 00:30:50,620
because the batteries only had a charge for about six hours.

535
00:30:50,720 --> 00:30:52,520
And if those batteries ran down, the Hubble

536
00:30:52,620 --> 00:30:53,760
would have been dead.

537
00:30:53,860 --> 00:30:56,120
So once the shuttle released its own power system

538
00:30:56,220 --> 00:30:58,600
from the Hubble, we had to get those solar panels out

539
00:30:58,700 --> 00:30:59,700
in about six hours.

540
00:30:59,800 --> 00:31:01,640
So we're all frightened about that.

541
00:31:01,740 --> 00:31:03,900
One of the solar panels stuck,

542
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:08,140
delaying separation and costing precious time.

543
00:31:08,240 --> 00:31:11,540
As the clock ticked down, Discovery's crew prepared

544
00:31:11,640 --> 00:31:13,140
for an emergency spacewalk.

545
00:31:13,141 --> 00:31:16,880
spacewalk.

546
00:31:16,881 --> 00:31:18,880
This is Mission Control Houston.

547
00:31:18,881 --> 00:31:21,540
Flight controllers here in Mission Control Center

548
00:31:21,541 --> 00:31:25,480
discussing an impending deadline.

549
00:31:25,481 --> 00:31:27,680
The other thing I need an answer to

550
00:31:27,681 --> 00:31:31,600
is if I can go ahead and commit the EVA with the thought

551
00:31:31,601 --> 00:31:33,660
of going out and cranking it out,

552
00:31:33,661 --> 00:31:36,060
if whatever they're about to do fails,

553
00:31:36,061 --> 00:31:38,560
but they want us to just press on to back them up,

554
00:31:38,561 --> 00:31:39,560
we need to get on with it.

555
00:31:39,561 --> 00:31:43,560
OK, flight, I'll come back with the answer.

556
00:31:43,561 --> 00:31:46,060
I need answers now.

557
00:31:46,061 --> 00:31:49,060
But we had the astronauts already getting their spacesuits on

558
00:31:49,061 --> 00:31:51,900
and getting ready for, to go outside and do a spacewalk,

559
00:31:51,901 --> 00:31:53,900
to hand crank them out just in case.

560
00:31:53,901 --> 00:31:56,940
And we got within one or two hours before power ran out,

561
00:31:56,941 --> 00:31:59,440
when the solar panels finally came out.

562
00:31:59,441 --> 00:32:02,520
Discovery, go for Hubble release.

563
00:32:02,521 --> 00:32:05,560
Spacecraft Hubble was free at last,

564
00:32:05,561 --> 00:32:10,940
at last, orbiting 380 miles above its home world.

565
00:32:10,941 --> 00:32:12,940
Aboard the space shuttle, astronauts

566
00:32:12,941 --> 00:32:15,840
celebrated the moment by recalling the telescope's

567
00:32:15,841 --> 00:32:17,680
namesake.

568
00:32:17,681 --> 00:32:20,800
We managed to obtain this device,

569
00:32:20,801 --> 00:32:24,320
which is the guiding eyepiece, we are assured,

570
00:32:24,321 --> 00:32:30,160
from the 100-inch telescope on which Edwin Hubble did many

571
00:32:30,161 --> 00:32:32,680
of his observations out at Mount Wilson in California.

572
00:32:32,681 --> 00:32:34,980
So it's his fundamental work, in fact.

573
00:32:34,981 --> 00:32:38,820
The eyes of the Earth were ready to behold the origins

574
00:32:38,920 --> 00:32:40,660
of the Big Bang.

575
00:32:40,760 --> 00:32:43,860
Who could have thought that this dream come true would soon

576
00:32:43,960 --> 00:32:46,720
become astronomy's worst nightmare?

577
00:32:57,800 --> 00:33:01,600
Almost immediately, the solar panels caused problems.

578
00:33:01,601 --> 00:33:05,880
Every 45 minutes, Hubble moved from sunlight to darkness

579
00:33:05,980 --> 00:33:09,840
with an abrupt temperature shift of hundreds of degrees.

580
00:33:09,940 --> 00:33:12,340
The sharp temperature changes caused the arrays

581
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:15,880
to contract or expand, shaking the delicate machine

582
00:33:15,980 --> 00:33:17,620
for several minutes.

583
00:33:17,720 --> 00:33:19,960
Hubble had a bad case of the jitters

584
00:33:20,060 --> 00:33:21,360
and no way to get rid of them.

585
00:33:24,820 --> 00:33:27,720
Tests over the next few weeks revealed an even more

586
00:33:27,820 --> 00:33:29,260
puzzling problem.

587
00:33:29,261 --> 00:33:32,960
from the 94-inch mirror simply didn't make sense.

588
00:33:33,060 --> 00:33:36,600
It seemed to indicate that the mirror could not focus.

589
00:33:36,700 --> 00:33:38,840
As the evidence mounted, no one was

590
00:33:38,940 --> 00:33:44,940
eager to confront the possibility of a major malfunction.

591
00:33:45,040 --> 00:33:47,640
The primary contractor who had built the mirror

592
00:33:47,740 --> 00:33:50,780
and the telescope, and who was nominally in charge of helping

593
00:33:50,880 --> 00:33:54,320
to set up and align the optics, was saying in this period

594
00:33:54,420 --> 00:33:56,020
that there wasn't a problem.

595
00:33:56,120 --> 00:33:58,920
So I was on the one hand saying there was a problem.

596
00:33:58,921 --> 00:34:00,580
The official contractor, on the other hand,

597
00:34:00,680 --> 00:34:02,560
was saying there wasn't a problem.

598
00:34:02,660 --> 00:34:05,020
And then all at once, the problem

599
00:34:05,120 --> 00:34:07,960
was too obvious to deny.

600
00:34:08,060 --> 00:34:12,400
A mistake in the primary mirror, an optical error 1 40th

601
00:34:12,500 --> 00:34:16,400
of the thickness of a human hair had left Hubble seriously

602
00:34:16,500 --> 00:34:17,640
flawed.

603
00:34:17,740 --> 00:34:21,940
The astronomical community was devastated.

604
00:34:22,040 --> 00:34:25,280
It was finally in late June that the final data came in,

605
00:34:25,380 --> 00:34:26,940
and we couldn't deny it.

606
00:34:26,941 --> 00:34:28,600
And it was just a crushing blow.

607
00:34:28,700 --> 00:34:31,180
I mean, I remember getting a call on a Sunday afternoon,

608
00:34:31,280 --> 00:34:33,280
and I was crushed.

609
00:34:33,380 --> 00:34:35,780
The Mirror disaster was immediately followed

610
00:34:35,880 --> 00:34:38,580
by a public relations fiasco.

611
00:34:38,680 --> 00:34:42,120
The press and public savaged NASA for its apparent hubris

612
00:34:42,220 --> 00:34:44,060
and incompetence.

613
00:34:44,160 --> 00:34:46,860
Coming just four years after the space shuttle Challenger

614
00:34:46,960 --> 00:34:51,100
tragedy, Hubble's plight made many wonder if NASA could do

615
00:34:51,200 --> 00:34:52,540
anything right.

616
00:34:52,640 --> 00:34:56,700
The summer of 1990 was filled with scathing editorials

617
00:34:56,701 --> 00:34:58,940
and brutal cartoons.

618
00:34:59,040 --> 00:35:02,100
The most complex scientific instrument ever built

619
00:35:02,200 --> 00:35:05,780
had become a national joke.

620
00:35:05,880 --> 00:35:08,240
Congress would reconvene in September

621
00:35:08,340 --> 00:35:14,060
amid fears that the lawmakers would simply let Hubble die.

622
00:35:14,160 --> 00:35:16,380
If Congress had turned off the telescope that summer

623
00:35:16,480 --> 00:35:18,560
before we had a chance to fix it,

624
00:35:18,660 --> 00:35:22,600
it would have been a death blow to American astronomy.

625
00:35:22,700 --> 00:35:25,560
We would probably never get a chance within our lifetime,

626
00:35:25,561 --> 00:35:27,960
certainly, to build another large optical telescope.

627
00:35:28,060 --> 00:35:30,600
And for those 10,000 people that had spent decades

628
00:35:30,700 --> 00:35:34,560
on this program, it would have been a personal tragedy.

629
00:35:34,660 --> 00:35:37,600
The impending tragedy threatened to consume everyone

630
00:35:37,700 --> 00:35:39,000
connected with it.

631
00:35:39,100 --> 00:35:41,380
As summer turned to fall in Washington,

632
00:35:41,480 --> 00:35:45,640
not only the Hubble telescope, but the future of NASA itself

633
00:35:45,740 --> 00:35:49,480
and America's space program hung in the balance.

634
00:35:49,481 --> 00:35:56,480
As the wounded Hubble telescope circled the Earth,

635
00:35:56,580 --> 00:36:00,300
an investigation traced its imperfection to human error.

636
00:36:00,400 --> 00:36:03,600
Computers had warned of problems with the mirror,

637
00:36:03,700 --> 00:36:07,860
warnings unaccountably ignored by optical supervisors.

638
00:36:07,960 --> 00:36:12,700
By a strange twist of fate, yet another human miscalculation

639
00:36:12,800 --> 00:36:15,800
would rescue Hubble.

640
00:36:15,900 --> 00:36:19,480
We were saved by Saddam Hussein before the Earth

641
00:36:19,481 --> 00:36:21,980
Before the invasion of Kuwait, there

642
00:36:21,981 --> 00:36:24,980
had been congressional and Senate hearings into it,

643
00:36:24,981 --> 00:36:26,520
and more promised in the fall.

644
00:36:26,521 --> 00:36:28,920
Well, then he invaded Kuwait, and I think

645
00:36:28,921 --> 00:36:30,800
that distracted the public opinion

646
00:36:30,801 --> 00:36:34,300
and gave us a window in which to fix the problem.

647
00:36:34,301 --> 00:36:36,940
In theory, Hubble's nearsightedness

648
00:36:36,941 --> 00:36:40,340
could be corrected by replacing its camera with one

649
00:36:40,341 --> 00:36:43,780
equipped with compensating lenses.

650
00:36:43,781 --> 00:36:46,180
A nickel-sized piece of corrective glass

651
00:36:46,181 --> 00:36:48,840
might restore its full vision.

652
00:36:48,841 --> 00:36:52,340
A shuttle crew of seven trained for nearly two years

653
00:36:52,440 --> 00:36:54,040
for the repair mission.

654
00:36:54,140 --> 00:36:57,040
They spent 400 hours in water tanks,

655
00:36:57,140 --> 00:37:02,380
practicing maneuvering Hubble's refrigerator-sized components.

656
00:37:02,480 --> 00:37:05,420
But for Story Musgrave and his fellow astronauts,

657
00:37:05,520 --> 00:37:08,260
nothing mattered more than mental preparation.

658
00:37:12,300 --> 00:37:17,640
It's choreographing a dance such that five days of activity

659
00:37:17,641 --> 00:37:21,440
out there, you know precisely what body motions you're

660
00:37:21,540 --> 00:37:24,840
going to take, how you're going to fit into things,

661
00:37:24,940 --> 00:37:26,940
where all the 300 tools are going

662
00:37:27,040 --> 00:37:29,080
to be throughout that five days.

663
00:37:29,180 --> 00:37:32,720
You simply build a script that you can now follow through out

664
00:37:32,820 --> 00:37:34,720
there in space.

665
00:37:34,820 --> 00:37:36,820
When the shuttle Endeavour blasted

666
00:37:36,920 --> 00:37:41,500
off in a spectacular night launch in December 1993,

667
00:37:41,600 --> 00:37:44,760
it embarked on an extraordinarily complex

668
00:37:44,761 --> 00:37:46,560
and high-risk mission.

669
00:37:46,660 --> 00:37:49,400
Five full nights of spacewalks were planned,

670
00:37:49,500 --> 00:37:51,600
by far the most ever.

671
00:37:51,700 --> 00:37:54,000
The astronauts were charged with attempting

672
00:37:54,100 --> 00:37:56,300
a dozen difficult tasks.

673
00:37:56,400 --> 00:37:59,100
Insiders would have been glad to see half the repairs

674
00:37:59,200 --> 00:38:03,580
performed successfully, all the while transmitting pictures

675
00:38:03,680 --> 00:38:06,340
back to spellbound Earthlings.

676
00:38:06,440 --> 00:38:10,460
Endeavour began by capturing Hubble on its long arm.

677
00:38:10,560 --> 00:38:12,560
The first order of business was replacing

678
00:38:12,561 --> 00:38:16,260
the troublesome solar arrays with new, temperature-resistant

679
00:38:16,360 --> 00:38:17,860
panels.

680
00:38:17,960 --> 00:38:21,360
When one of the old arrays refused to go quietly,

681
00:38:21,460 --> 00:38:24,500
it was unceremoniously ejected into space.

682
00:38:29,920 --> 00:38:33,880
Replacing the arrays had been built into Hubble's design

683
00:38:33,980 --> 00:38:36,480
and was a relatively simple procedure.

684
00:38:36,580 --> 00:38:38,980
However, no one had anticipated the need

685
00:38:38,981 --> 00:38:42,480
to replace the solar array drive electronics.

686
00:38:42,580 --> 00:38:47,320
That task would require exceptional patience.

687
00:38:47,420 --> 00:38:51,520
I have a very bulky space suit that weighs about 500 pounds

688
00:38:51,620 --> 00:38:55,800
with me in it, and I'm riding on the end of a 60-foot arm,

689
00:38:55,900 --> 00:38:58,700
dealing with screws that are three millimeters

690
00:38:58,800 --> 00:39:00,300
and are non-captive.

691
00:39:00,400 --> 00:39:03,100
That task was excruciatingly painful.

692
00:39:03,200 --> 00:39:06,480
That was three hours of very, very hard work

693
00:39:06,580 --> 00:39:08,580
with three millimeter screws.

694
00:39:08,581 --> 00:39:11,880
And the reason being is because that job had not designed

695
00:39:11,980 --> 00:39:14,920
to be done by a space walker.

696
00:39:15,020 --> 00:39:18,820
Day three marked the critical moment of the mission.

697
00:39:18,920 --> 00:39:21,500
To correct Hubble's vision, the primary camera

698
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:22,900
would be replaced.

699
00:39:23,000 --> 00:39:26,500
And that meant removing the mirror's protective cover.

700
00:39:26,600 --> 00:39:30,700
Human hands would be moving large, unwieldy objects

701
00:39:30,800 --> 00:39:35,100
just six inches from the mirror's surface.

702
00:39:35,101 --> 00:39:38,300
Even the slightest of touching on the Hubble service,

703
00:39:38,400 --> 00:39:41,300
you are making dust, which could end up on the optics.

704
00:39:41,400 --> 00:39:43,740
Every time you have to tie yourself down

705
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:47,240
or some other instrument and you put a metal hook,

706
00:39:47,340 --> 00:39:49,640
metal to metal, you're worried about flaking,

707
00:39:49,740 --> 00:39:51,860
you're worried about scarring.

708
00:39:51,960 --> 00:39:55,260
We were very, very concerned about the threat

709
00:39:55,360 --> 00:39:57,060
that we were to Hubble.

710
00:40:02,160 --> 00:40:04,200
When we got that camera in and they turned it on

711
00:40:04,201 --> 00:40:06,840
to make sure it was working, and it was working,

712
00:40:06,940 --> 00:40:09,000
that was a great, great, great relief.

713
00:40:09,100 --> 00:40:11,680
I think everything after that was sort of anti-colimatic.

714
00:40:11,780 --> 00:40:14,280
Getting that camera in was the number one priority,

715
00:40:14,380 --> 00:40:16,840
because that was the camera that was going to do all the science

716
00:40:16,940 --> 00:40:17,840
that we always promised.

717
00:40:29,660 --> 00:40:31,300
The Endeavour repair mission

718
00:40:31,301 --> 00:40:35,400
succeeded beyond NASA's wildest dreams.

719
00:40:35,500 --> 00:40:38,000
Every one of the dozen planned tasks

720
00:40:38,100 --> 00:40:40,540
was carried out flawlessly.

721
00:40:40,640 --> 00:40:43,340
With its vision restored, Hubble allowed humans

722
00:40:43,440 --> 00:40:47,540
to see 10 times more clearly than ever before,

723
00:40:47,640 --> 00:40:51,060
the same jump in resolution as when Galileo first pointed

724
00:40:51,160 --> 00:40:54,260
his spyglass towards the sky.

725
00:40:54,360 --> 00:41:00,160
This photo of galaxy M100 was taken by Hubble in 1990.

726
00:41:00,161 --> 00:41:05,560
Here, the same region viewed by the repaired telescope.

727
00:41:05,660 --> 00:41:08,400
It's a very, very exciting time to be doing astronomy.

728
00:41:08,500 --> 00:41:10,700
And everybody has their favorite objects,

729
00:41:10,800 --> 00:41:13,400
which they have studied for many years.

730
00:41:13,500 --> 00:41:15,200
And when you finally get the opportunity

731
00:41:15,300 --> 00:41:17,820
to take a Hubble picture of those objects,

732
00:41:17,920 --> 00:41:20,320
you'll suddenly see things that you hadn't suspected.

733
00:41:20,420 --> 00:41:22,120
You'll see things that you'd long suspected,

734
00:41:22,220 --> 00:41:23,820
but really wanted to prove.

735
00:41:23,920 --> 00:41:26,820
You'll always learn something new.

736
00:41:26,920 --> 00:41:29,280
Hubble has put some of astronomy's most

737
00:41:29,281 --> 00:41:32,480
most treasured imaginings to the test.

738
00:41:32,580 --> 00:41:35,080
What Hubble did was look at the center of a galaxy

739
00:41:35,180 --> 00:41:37,520
where we suspected there might be a black hole.

740
00:41:37,620 --> 00:41:40,020
A black hole is an object that is very, very dense

741
00:41:40,120 --> 00:41:43,320
and very, very small, so dense and so tiny

742
00:41:43,420 --> 00:41:45,320
that the speed of escape from that object

743
00:41:45,420 --> 00:41:46,840
exceeds the speed of light.

744
00:41:46,940 --> 00:41:48,240
So nothing can get out of it.

745
00:41:48,340 --> 00:41:49,640
Everything just gets sucked into it

746
00:41:49,740 --> 00:41:51,040
and disappears from the universe.

747
00:41:51,140 --> 00:41:55,040
Black holes have been fantasy and science fiction for decades.

748
00:41:55,140 --> 00:41:57,180
And what Hubble has done now is it's shown us

749
00:41:57,181 --> 00:42:02,480
It's shown us that black holes are scientific fact.

750
00:42:02,580 --> 00:42:06,720
Hubble was made possible by the 10,000 humans who labored

751
00:42:06,820 --> 00:42:11,520
so long to conceive, build, and repair it.

752
00:42:11,620 --> 00:42:14,960
But even more, it is the direct descendant of those men whose

753
00:42:15,060 --> 00:42:18,700
thirst for knowledge would not be denied.

754
00:42:18,800 --> 00:42:22,300
Galileo sacrificing his freedom to put the sun at the center

755
00:42:22,400 --> 00:42:24,140
of our universe.

756
00:42:24,141 --> 00:42:29,240
George Hale overcoming his demons to build ever greater

757
00:42:29,340 --> 00:42:34,420
observatories, and Edwin Hubble guiding a telescope for eight

758
00:42:34,520 --> 00:42:38,820
hours in a freezing dome to capture evidence

759
00:42:38,920 --> 00:42:42,760
of an expanding universe.

760
00:42:42,860 --> 00:42:46,420
People really yearn to understand the stars, the universe,

761
00:42:46,520 --> 00:42:47,900
because that's where we came from.

762
00:42:48,000 --> 00:42:50,740
We are children of the stars in the sense

763
00:42:50,741 --> 00:42:55,840
that the chemical elements in our bodies were cooked in stars.

764
00:42:55,940 --> 00:42:59,280
The carbon, the calcium, all the good stuff in our bodies

765
00:42:59,380 --> 00:43:01,980
were cooked inside the nuclear furnaces of stars.

766
00:43:02,080 --> 00:43:04,680
And those stars blew up and sent those chemicals

767
00:43:04,780 --> 00:43:06,180
all over the universe.

768
00:43:06,280 --> 00:43:08,060
And luckily, some of them collected here in the solar

769
00:43:08,160 --> 00:43:09,920
system.

770
00:43:10,020 --> 00:43:14,760
Mankind has always been a race of stargazers.

771
00:43:14,860 --> 00:43:16,440
The tools change.

772
00:43:16,540 --> 00:43:19,160
The yearning remains the same.

773
00:43:19,161 --> 00:43:21,160
to know whence we came.

774
00:43:21,161 --> 00:43:23,960
Writing in 1902, at the dawn of our great age

775
00:43:23,961 --> 00:43:25,960
of astronomical discovery,

776
00:43:25,961 --> 00:43:31,000
H.G. Wells foresaw the adventure that lay ahead.

777
00:43:31,001 --> 00:43:34,920
It is possible that all the human mind has ever accomplished

778
00:43:34,921 --> 00:43:38,200
is but the dream before the awakening.

779
00:43:38,201 --> 00:43:41,960
A day will come when beings who are now latent in our thoughts

780
00:43:41,961 --> 00:43:45,560
and hiding in our minds shall stand upon this earth

781
00:43:45,561 --> 00:43:47,560
as one stands upon a footstool,

